Design verdicts vs lingo-db (2026-06-10):
- Whole-pipeline compile (scan/filter/sink in one function): NOT replicated
  inside the DuckDB adapter — DuckDB's PipelineExecutor owns morsel-parallel
  scan and sink combine/finalize; we can only intercept at operator
  boundaries. The fused operator segment (filter + N probes + projection)
  captures lingo-db's main win. A true scan-to-sink compile requires owning
  the runtime (see "next-level sql-jit runtime" plan in CLAUDE.md).
- 2048-row output cap with bail vs lingo-db's growing buffers: bail kept.
  DuckDB's contract is 2048-row chunks + HAVE_MORE_OUTPUT iteration;
  overflow only occurs for non-unique build keys, the bail path restores
  state, and the interpreter re-emits in chunks. Growing buffers would add
  a copy back into chunks for no benefit.

## Query-JIT (--jit-level=query): Phases 0-4 COMPLETE
Status: COMPLETE and gated
Date: 2026-06-10/11
Source: lingo-db handleExecutionStepCPU (one function per execution step,
  SubOpToControlFlow.cpp:4334-4365) + SplitIntoExecutionSteps; chained HT
  with single-threaded finalize after per-worker appends (Hashtable.h
  merge model); morsel-driven parallel-for with per-thread partial state.
Description: The "next-level sql-jit runtime" from the 2026-06-10 design
  verdict, implemented. Per sub-query, the adapter Prepares (DuckDB
  optimizer) -> ConvertPlanToIR -> AnnotateBuildSides (walks the physical
  plan, records build/probe sides into new SimplestJoin::build_child —
  submodule change; closes the swapped-side gap by construction for
  query-jit) -> AnalyzeQueryJit/BuildExecutionSteps
  (src/qjit/query_jit_steps.cpp, three-pass; tree-walk Decompose: probe
  spine streaming, build children spawn HT_BUILD steps, DFS post-order) ->
  CompileQuerySteps (src/jit/ir_to_llvm.cpp:7743, ONE LLVM module + ONE
  entry fn, one outlined morsel body per step, runtime calls
  qjit_parallel_for / qjit_ht_finalize) -> QjitExecutor::Run over
  QjitWorkerPool (own pool with stable worker ids; util ThreadPool has
  none). Any reject => whole sub-query on the DuckDB interpreter
  (per-sub-query fallback, [AQP-QJIT] accept/reject traces).
Key properties:
  - Fresh IR per sub-query inside the adapter => IR and physical plan share
    one binder; the stale-IR/table-index mismatches that plagued
    pipeline-jit (see Table Index Mismatch Fix above) do not apply.
  - Flag is bit 7 (AQP_JIT_QUERY_JIT), deliberately NOT in
    AQP_JIT_LEVEL_MASK (that mask gates the DuckDB-embedded RegisterJIT
    path, which query-jit bypasses).
  - Scan layer: StoragePlan FlatTables (one-time columnar load of base
    tables at process start; INT32 zero-copy, VARCHAR string_t views into
    the string pool, memoized). Temps: dual-materialized — QjitTable in
    qjit_temps_ for compiled consumers (POSITIONAL column resolution;
    chunk attr names can be "colN" placeholders) + CDC StoredTempResult
    for interpreter consumers; fallback-produced temps converted
    CDC->QjitTable on demand. (Dual materialization is killed by Phase 5
    §5.7 scan_qjit_temp.)
  - NULL semantics: inner-join-only v1 — NULL keys skipped at build, NULL
    probe keys skip lookup, payload NULLs via validity prefix + zero slots
    + shared zero global for varchar; sinks deep-copy varchar into arena.
  - v1 coverage: Scan/Chunk/Filter/Projection/inner Join/ungrouped
    MIN/MAX/SUM/COUNT; dtypes INT32/INT64/VARCHAR.
Gates passed (2026-06-11):
  - All 113 JOB queries x 3 splits golden (configs permanent in
    correctness_test.sh).
  - ASan clean modulo the known 4263-byte libLLVM leak.
  - Thread-count invariance (--query-jit-threads=1 == default).
  - RSS flat across --benchmark repeats (plateau verified on 16b and 6f).
Reject distribution on JOB (final): 99x expr:single-attr (mark-join
  IN-lists — Phase 5 §5.2 target), 3x plan-empty-result, 1x exception
  (pre-existing 10c converter cast).

## 2026-06-11 Pre-Phase-5 verification + rulings session

1. --jit-cache semantics (verified in code, ir_to_llvm.cpp Impl:358-416):
   the flag gates the WHOLE in-memory object cache (Impl::cache_enabled).
   OFF (default) = zero reuse of any kind; ON = repeat hits AND
   cross-query hits share one FNV-1a content-hash map (cannot be gated
   separately; constants are part of the key so cross-query hits are
   rare). No disk persistence exists — never reuses across runs. README
   --jit-cache row was stale (claimed disk cache + path option) — fixed.
2. Storage-plan ruling (verified): only TWO consumers — kernel-path
   (CSR/sorted/dimension, gated kernel_path != NONE) and query-jit
   (FlatTable column data ONLY; zero CSR/index references in src/qjit/).
   expr/operator/pipeline never read it; measure scripts now pass
   --storage-plan --storage-cache only for jit-level=query. Query-jit
   pre-builds NOTHING per query; the cache file only avoids redoing the
   one-time startup scan. CSR/index sections in the file are dead weight
   for query-jit => Phase 5 §5.6 trim.
3. Timing audit (user ruling: loading excluded everywhere): DB open / CSV
   load / storage-plan load+build are timed only into prepare_middleware,
   which is written ONLY in non-benchmark mode => excluded from all
   plotted benchmark CSVs. Benchmark per-repeat column 0 is
   ResetQueryState() time (0.2-0.7 ms) — plot_middleware_jit.py had it
   mislabeled "prepare_middleware"; renamed to "reset" (15 occurrences).
   All 15 split x jit-level CSV layouts re-verified to parse through the
   edited script (13a, --repeat=15).
4. Spec-jit wait-policy study (user ruling: WAIT on match-but-not-ready,
   ALL jit levels; on true MISS query-jit falls through to inline
   TryCompileQueryJit — spec strictly additive):
   - Taxonomy: per not-ready event with remaining compile r, jit exec j,
     interp exec e: WAIT = r+j; NEVER-WAIT = e; HYBRID(X) = r+j if r<=X
     else X+e. HYBRID's give-up branch costs X+e >= e, so HYBRID never
     strictly beats both extremes on any event — algebra, no data needed.
   - 5-heavy-query probe (/tmp/waitpol, node-based, spec off): compiles
     0.3-5 ms/sub-query (expr/operator), 5-16 ms (pipeline/query); spec
     overlap window only ~2-10 ms (launch is post-execute by design) =>
     not-ready common; never-wait would also poison learned-miss history.
   - Full-JOB confirmation (113 queries, 565 spec-eligible sub-queries,
     node-based): expr/operator/pipeline with spec ON (waits embedded in
     execute col) — WAIT beats NEVER-WAIT by +478/+447/+426 ms suite-wide;
     worst per-event losses ~10 ms, all with jit_compile~0 (spec ready =>
     both policies run jit; loss is codegen, not waiting). Query-jit
     (spec off, 3 repeats): worst case (zero overlap) WAIT loses -3.7 s,
     best case (full overlap) wins +0.9 s.
5. NEW FINDING — query-jit slower-than-interpreter population: 137/565
   sub-queries execute SLOWER compiled than interpreted (sum loss 1.6 s
   vs sum gain 2.5 s; worst by sum j-e: 8c 183 ms, 17a 116 ms, 9d 86 ms,
   7c 84 ms, 16b 80 ms, 12a 66 ms, 33a 62, 13d 57, 12c 52, 22d 49). No
   wait policy fixes j>e; investigation is Phase 5 §5.5 (codegen fix vs
   cost-based bailout = user decision).
6. scan_qjit_temp dependency check (corrects an earlier design note): the
   precedents scan_temp_collection/scan_kernel_temp live entirely in the
   MIDDLEWARE adapter (RegisterTempCollectionScan,
   duckdb_adapter.cpp:161-324; public DuckDB API: catalog
   CreateTableFunction + db_config.replacement_scans). §5.7 therefore
