## Archived: Phase 5 design specs (moved from CLAUDE.md, 2026-06-12)

Original design text for §5.1-§5.7, all implemented and gated (results
in "Query-JIT Phase 5 COMPLETE" above). Kept verbatim for the rationale
data (wait-policy measurements, cache-key design, ABI notes).

### 5.1 Object cache for query-jit + pipeline-jit fusion compilers

`--jit-cache` is a DEBUG flag, default OFF (final measurements compile from
scratch every repeat). Semantics of the existing cache (ir_to_llvm.cpp
Impl:358-416, keep them): in-memory `obj_cache` map keyed by FNV-1a content
hash → object-code bytes; process lifetime; survives `ResetModules()`
(bytes re-added via `addObjectFile`); reuse hits across repeats AND across
different queries/sub-queries whenever compile content is byte-identical
(constants are part of the key — no parameterization, so cross-query hits
are rare in practice; repeats 2..N of the same sub-query are the hit
population). The flag gates the WHOLE cache (Impl::cache_enabled): when
OFF there is zero reuse of any kind — repeats and cross-query alike both
compile from scratch; the two hit populations share one map and cannot be
gated separately.

- Query-jit: add `TryCacheLoad`/`pending_cache_key` to `CompileQuerySteps`
  (:7743). Key = FNV-1a over a canonical serialization of `QjitQueryPlan`:
  per step (source table/temp name + is_temp, col refs + dtypes, filter
  expr text, projection list, join key dtypes + layout offsets, sink kind +
  ht id), HT tuple sizes/layouts, agg descs + output cells, step order,
  plus codegen flags (skip_opt_, simd). Row counts EXCLUDED — verified the
  module is row-count independent (`nrows`/`morsel_size` are runtime loads
  from ctx, ir_to_llvm.cpp:8224-8237). The entry name must switch from
  `s_filter_counter` to hash-derived (`"qjit_query_c" + key.substr(0,12)`,
  pattern at :4952) or cache hits can't look the symbol up. No
  duplicate-symbol hazard: TryCompileQueryJit calls `ResetModules()` before
  every compile (duckdb_adapter.cpp:2723) ⇒ exactly one query-jit module
  per generation.
- Pipeline-jit fusion compilers (the old "Phase 6"): same
  TryCacheLoad/pending_cache_key protocol for
  `CompileFilterProbeProjectFusion` (:5719) and `CompileMultiProbeChain`
  (:6732); key over stage IR text + schemas + per-stage flags
  (skip_hash_cmp, payload prune, batch_probe, prefetch) via the existing
  `BuildCacheContent` helper; hash-derived fn names as above.
- Gate: `--jit-cache` repeat-2+ compile time ≈ 0 in the jit_compile CSV
  column; goldens unchanged with cache on AND off.

### 5.2 Mark-join IN-list support (`expr:single-attr`, 99 sub-queries)

Verified shape (20a): DuckDB rewrites large IN-lists into a MARK hash join
against a `CHUNK_GET` constant table; the IR converter emits
`FilterNode` with qual `SingleAttrExpr` (Bool, table_index = the mark
join's index, printed "IN (...)") above a `JoinNode(Mark)` whose build
child is a `ChunkNode` with non-empty `GetContents()`. (Small IN-lists are
already plain `InExprNode` quals — whitelisted and compiled.) Affected: 33
none / 46 node-based / 20 rel-center sub-queries (14a-c, 18b-c, 20a-c,
21a-c, ...).

Fix — rewrite to an in-set predicate, NO hash join at all (strictly better
than DuckDB's mark join here):
- In `query_jit_steps.cpp` (both Analyzer and Decompose; no IR mutation —
  recognize the pattern in place): a single-attr Bool qual whose
  table_index matches a Mark `JoinNode` on the spine, where the join's
  build child is a constants-only ChunkNode (`GetContents()` non-empty)
  and there is exactly one equi-condition ⇒ treat as a streaming filter op
  on the probe spine: filter column = the probe-side condition attr,
  constant set = the chunk contents column. Drop the join and the chunk
  from the steps (no HT id allocated).
- Codegen: reuse the existing `InExprNode` path — `aqp_in_set_i32/_i64/
  _str` runtime helpers are already registered in the shared LLJIT
  (Impl ctor, ir_to_llvm.cpp:476-478).
- NULL semantics are equivalent: NULL probe key ⇒ mark column NULL ⇒
  filter drops the row; NULL key IN (list) ⇒ NULL ⇒ drops. Inner-join-only
  invariant of the runtime is preserved (mark join never reaches it).
- Still reject (trace new reasons): negated single-attr (NOT IN /
  anti-mark), mark joins whose build child is a named temp chunk or
  another subtree, multi-condition mark joins.
- Gate: the 99 sub-queries flip to `exec` traces; goldens ×3.

### 5.3 Spec-jit for query-jit (wait-on-not-ready kept, both jit levels)

Current spec-jit (pipeline-jit only, node-based only,
ir_query_splitter.cpp:715-1014): after Execute(i) finishes, a 1-worker bg
pool Prepares sub-query i+1 on a separate Connection (placeholder temp
registered with the ACTUAL cardinality of temp i — launch is deliberately
post-execute, :722-729, so DuckDB's join order is accurate) and runs
RegisterJITImpl into a speculative AQPJITContext; Phase B
(PrecomputeNextExtraction) keeps or relaunches after the real SplitIR; HIT
(CheckSpeculativeResult :1110) = normalized-SQL match + est/actual
cardinality ratio ≤ 2 guard; learned-miss history skips known-miss
launches on later repeats. Query-jit currently CANNOT use it: both launch
sites gate on `jit_flags & AQP_JIT_LEVEL_MASK` (bit 7 is excluded), and
the mechanism compiles DuckDB-embedded pipeline fns + executes the
speculative prepared statement through DuckDB — query-jit does neither.

Design (user ruled 2026-06-11, from measurement):
- **WAIT on match-but-not-ready, ALL jit levels** — keep
  CheckSpeculativeResult's blocking behavior (:1132-1139) and give
  query-jit the same semantics. Data basis (5 heaviest JOB queries,
  node-based, spec off, /tmp/waitpol): compiles are 0.3-5 ms/sub-query
  (expr/operator) and 5-16 ms (pipeline/query), while the bg-compile
  overlap window is only the inter-sub-query middleware work + Prepare
  (~2-10 ms — spec launches post-execute by design), so not-ready is
  common; the wait residual is bounded by one compile (≤16 ms, and ≤
  inline recompile by construction), whereas never-wait forfeits
  jit-vs-interp deltas of up to ~50 ms/sub-query, randomizes the
  execution path (timing race → nondeterministic repeats), and would
  poison the learned-miss history (not-ready counted as miss ⇒ later
  repeats permanently interpret).
  Full-JOB confirmation (113 queries × 565 spec-eligible sub-queries,
  node-based, 2026-06-11): expr/operator/pipeline with spec ON (waits
  embedded in the execute column) — WAIT beats NEVER-WAIT in aggregate by
  +478/+447/+426 ms suite-wide; worst per-event losses are ~10 ms and ALL
  have jit_compile≈0 (spec was ready ⇒ both policies run jit there — the
  loss is codegen, not waiting). Query-jit (spec off, /tmp/policy_full/
  query_node_r3.csv): worst case (zero overlap, r=full compile, Σr=4.6 s)
  WAIT loses −3.7 s; best case (full overlap) wins +0.9 s. The dominant
  term is NOT wait time but the 137/565 sub-queries whose compiled binary
  runs SLOWER than the interpreter (Σ loss 1.6 s vs Σ gain 2.5 s; worst:
  8c 183 ms, 17a 116 ms, 9d 86 ms, 7c 84 ms, 16b 80 ms, 12a 66 ms) — no
  wait policy fixes j>e; that population needs a §5.5 codegen/cost
  investigation, orthogonal to the policy ruling.
- **MISS ⇒ same as no-spec mode** (query-jit): on a true miss
  (SQL mismatch / ratio guard), fall through to the normal inline
  TryCompileQueryJit path, exactly like pipeline-jit recompiles inline on
  miss today. Spec stays strictly additive: never slower than spec-off.
- **Stale-plan / join-reorder check**: (a) launch only post-execute with
  actual cardinality in the placeholder (existing) so the speculative
  Prepare sees the same stats an inline Prepare would — same join order by
  construction; (b) normalized-SQL compare catches cross-sub-plan range
  predicate differences (bloom filters don't change SQL and are irrelevant
  to query-jit's own runtime); (c) keep the est/actual ratio > 2 guard as
  belt-and-braces.
- Query-jit bg task (new branch next to the pipeline one, same rotating
  `spec_compilers_` + ResetModules-at-task-start discipline on the
  1-worker pool): spec Connection → Prepare(spec_sql) → ConvertPlanToIR →
  AnnotateBuildSides → AnalyzeQueryJit → BuildExecutionSteps →
  CompileQuerySteps. Store `QjitQueryPlan` + entry fn + output/agg
  metadata in the SpeculativeCompilation. Do NOT resolve sources in the bg
  task — the consumed temp doesn't exist yet (placeholder has schema
  only); resolution (`ResolveSource`/`GetOrLoadQjitTemp`/
  `ResolveTempSource`) happens at HIT time on the main thread, after the
  real temp is stored.
- Gates to relax: `duckdb_flags = jit_flags & AQP_JIT_LEVEL_MASK; if (!duckdb_flags) return;`
  in LaunchSpeculativeCompile (:735) and PrecomputeNextExtraction (:873)
  must also accept `AQP_JIT_QUERY_JIT`. Keep the node-based-only strategy
  gate for v1 (relationship-center extension is a separate decision).
- Lifetime: DrainSpecs before temp clears (existing); the spec compiler's
  modules stay alive through the HIT execution (two-compiler rotation
  guarantees the next bg compile resets the OTHER instance); pool is
  quiescent when Run returns.
- Gate: goldens ×3 with spec on; `[AQP-SPECJIT]` HIT/MISS/not-ready counts
  per query; node-based perf with spec on vs off — jit_compile column ≈ 0
  on HIT sub-queries.

### 5.4 Parallel CAS hash-table insert

Today `QjitHashTable`: per-worker append buffers (race-free appends),
then `qjit_ht_finalize` single-threaded — counts entries, allocates the
pow-2 directory, walks all fragments linking each entry into its bucket
chain. The link walk is O(total rows) on one core (e.g. 6f temp builds,
millions of rows).

Change: keep per-worker appends; split finalize into (1) main thread sums
per-worker counts + allocates the directory; (2) `ParallelFor` over the
per-worker fragment lists — for each entry: hash → bucket → CAS push
(`e->next = head` then `compare_exchange_weak(head, e)` with release on
success / relaxed on failure, retry loop). Probe-side loads need no
atomics: the pool's condition-variable join at the end of the finalize
ParallelFor establishes happens-before for every subsequent probe step.
- ABI change: `qjit_ht_finalize(ht)` → `qjit_ht_finalize(ctx, ht)` (the
  runtime needs the pool); update the entry-fn emission
  (ir_to_llvm.cpp:8248) and the registered symbol.
- Chain order inside a bucket becomes nondeterministic. Safe: the probe
  loop emits ALL matches (multi-match backtracking), temp consumers are
  order-insensitive, and JOB finals are MIN aggregates — golden VALUES
  unchanged. The thread-invariance gate compares values, not row order.
- Gate: unit test — builds with N∈{1,2,16} workers produce identical probe
  result SETS (sort before compare); goldens ×3; finalize wall time before
  vs after on a heavy build (6f temp5, 3.7M rows).

### 5.5 Measurement + query-jit slower-than-interpreter investigation

`bash measure_breakdown_time_job.sh duckdb <split> query none` per split
(script already passes `--storage-plan --storage-cache` for query);
rectangular-CSV check (existing awk); per-query medians vs pipeline-jit and
interpreter; one cache-on debug run to confirm repeat-2+ compile ≈ 0.
Correctness configs for query are already permanent in correctness_test.sh.

Investigation (from the 2026-06-11 full-JOB policy run, node-based, see
§5.3): 137/565 sub-queries execute SLOWER compiled than interpreted
(Σ loss 1.6 s vs Σ gain 2.5 s). Worst queries by pure exec loss (Σ j−e,
ms): 8c 183, 17a 116, 9d 86, 7c 84, 16b 80, 12a 66, 33a 62, 13d 57,
12c 52, 22d 49. Profile these (e.g. 8c sub2: jit 138 ms vs interp 61 ms)
to find the cause — candidates: single-vs-multi-threaded sections,
HT finalize (§5.4 helps), missing scan-level selectivity tricks (zone
maps), morsel size, string handling. Decide per finding: codegen fix vs a
cost-based per-sub-query bailout (bailout shape/threshold is a USER
decision). Raw data was /tmp/policy_full/query_node_r3.csv (volatile);
regenerate with the measure script or a 3-repeat benchmark run.

### 5.6 Storage-cache trim for query-jit (skip CSR/index sections)

Query-jit pre-builds NOTHING per query: the storage plan is only a one-time
columnar load of the base tables (FlatTables) out of imdb.db via a DuckDB
scan; the binary cache file (`--storage-cache`) just avoids redoing that
scan on later runs. The CSR/sorted/inverted indexes in the same file are
kernel-path-only. Change (load AND build sides, gated on
`kernel_path == NONE` so kernel-path runs are untouched):
- `StoragePlan::LoadFromFile` gains a `skip_indexes` mode — seek past the
  CSR/sorted/inverted sections instead of materializing them (RAM + load
  time win; the cache file format is unchanged).
- First-run build path (aqp_middleware.cpp:414-430): skip
  `BuildCSRIndexes`/`BuildSortedIndices`/`BuildInvertedIndices` when only
  query-jit needs the plan; `SaveToFile` then writes empty index sections
  (a kernel-path run against such a cache must detect the empty sections
  and rebuild — or use a separate cache file; keep the two paths on
  separate `--storage-cache` paths to avoid surprises).
- Gate: query-jit goldens ×3 splits unchanged; RSS at first query
  noticeably lower than with full cache load; kernel-path configs still
  golden against their own (full) cache file.

### 5.7 `scan_qjit_temp` replacement scan (kill dual temp materialization)

No storage-plan dependency, and NO patched-DuckDB change / libduckdb
rebuild (verified: the precedents `scan_temp_collection` and
`scan_kernel_temp` are registered entirely in the MIDDLEWARE adapter via
DuckDB's public API — `RegisterTempCollectionScan`,
duckdb_adapter.cpp:161-203: `Catalog::CreateTableFunction` + a callback
appended to `db_config.replacement_scans`; the replacement-scan dispatch
that maps temp names → table functions is `TempCollectionReplacementScan`
:288-324). Today every query-jit-produced temp is stored twice: QjitTable
(compiled consumers) + row-converted CDC `StoredTempResult` (interpreter
consumers). Change:
- New table function `scan_qjit_temp` registered next to
  `scan_kernel_temp` (same pattern: bind reads schema from the adapter's
  `qjit_temps_` map via a `function_info` pointer, projection_pushdown
  on); scan fabricates 2048-row DataChunks on demand (INT32/INT64 direct,
  VARCHAR via string_t views into the QjitTable arena — arena outlives
  the scan because temps are cleared only at per-query reset,
  duckdb_adapter.cpp:2204).
- Add a third branch in `TempCollectionReplacementScan` checking
  `qjit_temps_`; delete the CDC write-back in the query-jit store path
  (StoreTempCollection's qjit branch). Fallback-produced temps
  (interpreter sub-queries) keep their CDC path unchanged.
- Cardinality: provide table_function cardinality from QjitTable nrows so
  the optimizer sees the same stats the CDC path gave (join order parity —
  verify with plan dumps on a split-heavy query, e.g. 16b). Note the CDC
  path also serves per-column stats (`TempCollectionStatistics` :275) —
  decide whether scan_qjit_temp needs equivalent stats or plan dumps show
  parity without them.
- Gate: goldens ×3 splits; RSS during 6f/16b drops vs dual
  materialization; mixed jit/fallback chains still correct (fallback
  consumer scanning a qjit temp, qjit consumer scanning a fallback temp).

