## Spec-jit redesign decision (2026-06-12) — full-JOB model, §6.6 DECIDED

### Instrumentation bug + fix (landed)

Bug: CheckSpeculativeResult's blocking `future.wait()` on
match-but-not-ready and the end-of-loop DrainSpecs both ran in an
UNTIMED gap (after the "generate sub-SQL" chrono_toc in
ir_query_splitter.cpp, before the adapter's chrono_tic), so spec-ON CSV
column sums underreported wall time by the full wait. Proof by
wall-clock equality: 10a spec wall 4.08 s vs nospec 4.04 s over 20 reps
while colsums differed by 710 ms; 26b hid 0.94 s over 20 reps. After
fix: 10a warm colsum 37.8→75.3 ms, 26b 73.1→125.6 ms; fixed overheads
match nospec (2.17 vs 2.18 s). Consequences: every prior node-based
spec-ON CSV (incl. Phase 6 job_result query CSVs) is tainted; 26b
actually LOSES to interp (≈120 vs 73.7 ms warm); the 2026-06-11
"WAIT beats NEVER-WAIT by 478 ms" ruling was an artifact of WAIT hiding
its time. rc/none splits unaffected (no spec).

Fix mechanics:
- ir_query_splitter.h: `spec_wait_us_` member; `DrainSpecs(bool
  charge_wait=false)`.
- ir_query_splitter.cpp: not-ready wait timed and charged via
  `DuckDBAdapter::AddSpecWaitTime`; end-of-loop `DrainSpecs(true)`;
  summary line gains `wait_ms=`; per-query reset now also clears
  spec_card_misses_ (pre-existing leak) and spec_wait_us_; --debug
  traces added: per-iter `prepare_est=`/`actual_card=` (the agreed
  §6.6(e) est/actual instrumentation), launch type, `waited X ms`,
  decision.
- duckdb_adapter.{h,cpp}: `spec_wait_extra_us_` +
  `AddSpecWaitTime`/`ConsumeSpecWaitUs`; ALL jit-column writes
  (ExecuteSQL qjit/pipeline/skipped, ExecuteSQLandCreateTempTable ×3,
  ExecuteSpeculativeAndCreateTempTable entry) fold the pending wait in;
  ResetQueryState clears it.
- Stale pre-execute-launch comments rewritten (hook install, Phase A
  header + !post_execute early-return, Phase B keep-on-match,
  ExecuteOneIteration launch remark, duckdb_adapter post-execute hook).

### Model (all 113 JOB, node-based + query-jit)

Inputs: nospec_full.csv / spec_full.csv / interp_full.csv (repeat=15,
warm medians) + spec_trace.out (--debug repeat=3, last-repeat events).
Model: /tmp/awork/model_designs.py (copy if needed). Per-iteration
quantities: c=inline compile, ej=jit exec, ei=interp exec, overlap
windows from measured mat/upd/ext/gen columns; launch type
(peek/phaseB/peek_kept), est/actual ratio, range-injection from traces.
Fast tier assumption: compile/10, exec×1.5 (sensitivity below).

  design                                          total    beats-interp
  INTERP                                         10.95 s        —
  NOSPEC (inline full compile)                   12.43 s        —
  CURRENT spec-jit (measured, fixed)             12.40 s      39/113
  OLD (early launch + relaunch on est-miss)      13.18 s      35/113
  compensate=false (miss → interp, no wait)      11.32 s      33/113
  compensate=true (miss → fast recompile)         9.58 s      69/113
  REFINED (+ oracle wait-vs-interp on not-ready)  8.80 s      83/113
  FASTALL (fast-compile everything, NO spec)      8.38 s        —
  FASTALL + oracle jit-vs-interp per iter         7.38 s        —
  triple oracle (early-full | fast | interp)      6.38 s        —

Sensitivity over fast-ratio {5,10,20} × penalty {1.2,1.5,2.0}:
REFINED 8.24–9.73 s (always best spec design, always beats interp);
compensate=true 8.80–11.21 s; cost model WITHOUT fast tier: 11.04 s
(loses to interp); FASTALL degrades to ~11.4 s at 5×/2.0 (more
penalty-sensitive than REFINED, which keeps full-quality code on the
usable path). Suite compile=7.04 s vs exec=4.56 s (nospec) —
node-based query-jit is COMPILE-dominated.

Trace stats (3 repeats): 1353 HIT / 0 MISS / 0 CARD_MISS decisions
(learned history + Phase B keep-or-relaunch catch mismatches before
consume; 949 peek launches, 765 Phase-B relaunches, 588 peek-kept),
215 RANGE-SQL events. Usability misses are NOT the cost center —
waits are.

Notable per-query (CURRENT → REFINED vs interp, ms): 29c 176→78 (92),
29a 156→62 (75), 29b 156→58 (59), 28b 148→61 (68), 33a 147→62 (53).
Worst residual REFINED losses vs interp: 13b 70 vs 46, 13c 66 vs 42,
1c 31 vs 10 (compile-dominated short queries — exactly what the fast
tier fixes beyond what spec can).

### Verdict + plan (now in CLAUDE.md §6.6)

Current spec-jit ≈ nospec (saves nothing; post-execute launch never
overlaps real compile time). OLD design measured worse — historical
removal confirmed. The user's compensate idea only pays with a fast
recompile tier; the decisive lever overall is the fast tier itself:
FASTALL with no speculation beats oracle-REFINED. Plan: (1) implement
fast-tier backend (CodeGenOptLevel::None + FastISel, no new dep; flag
in §5.1 cache key), measure real ratio/penalty; (2) re-model and pick
FASTALL-style vs REFINED compensate-jit; (3) TPDE only if FastISel
ratio <~5×; rename --spec-jit → --compensate-jit with the new design;
(4) re-measure tainted node-based query CSVs.

### Addendum (2026-06-12, user-ratified plan refinement)

User Q&A clarified and ratified the §6.6 plan: FASTALL = policy
(fast-compile everything inline, planned flag `--fast-compile`);
FastISel-O0 and TPDE = the two candidate BACKENDS for its fast tier —
FastISel first (no dependency), TPDE compared head-to-head under the
same policy only if FastISel's measured ratio <~5×. NEW_TRUE hard-
depends on the fast backend (its miss path IS a fast recompile);
NEW_FALSE alone loses to interp (11.32 s) so it exists only as
compensate=false. End state: per-query winner tuning among
interp/FASTALL/NEW_FALSE/NEW_TRUE (Phase-7 config-JSON pattern), plus
the early-launch-fast-compile hybrid (miss cost ≈ a wasted 0.5-1 ms
fast compile) as the path toward the ~6.3 s triple-oracle ceiling.
CLAUDE.md §6.6 fully rewritten to be self-contained for new sessions.

## §6.6 steps 1-3 EXECUTED (2026-06-12) — fast tier measured, TPDE wins

### Implementation (landed, goldens ×3 splits ×2 backends PASS)

`--fast-compile[=fastisel|tpde|off]` (param_config), plumbed via
DuckDBAdapter::SetFastCompile → IrToLlvmCompiler(FastCompileBackend)
and the splitter's spec compilers. FASTISEL = jtmb CodeGenOptLevel::None
+ EnableFastISel (lingo-db LLVMBackends.cpp:248-249 equivalent); both
fast modes also skip OptimiseModule (folded into skip_opt_). Backend
tag "F0/F1/F2" serialized into ALL 7 §5.1 cache-key sites. TPDE wiring:
LLJITBuilder::setCompileFunctionCreator → our `TpdeOrcCompiler`
(ir_to_llvm.cpp, after the using-declarations) — a reimplementation of
tpde_llvm::ConcurrentOrcCompiler whose LLVM-SimpleCompiler fallback is
NOT silent: it logs "[AQP-JIT] TPDE rejected module ..." so a
measurement run cannot secretly degrade to all-LLVM. Measured: ZERO
fallbacks across 113 queries × 3 splits — TPDE compiles 100% of our
query-jit modules.

TPDE build: vendored at third_party/tpde (copy of lingo-db's
vendored/tpde minus docs/.git; encodegen main.cpp patched to init BOTH
x64+arm64 LLVM targets — lingo-db never builds tpde-llvm so the bug
went unnoticed there). Built with clang-20 (needs <format>; g++-12
fails) + PIC; shipped as third_party/tpde_build/libtpde_bundle.so
linked with version script tpde_llvm.map (exports ONLY tpde_llvm::*;
everything else local). Rationale: lingo-db's prebuilt librunner.a
embeds its own tpde core (436 tpde:: symbols, baseline backend) — a
static-lib or ld-r+objcopy bundle hits multiple-definition /
COMDAT-relocation errors; the shared lib + version script is the only
clean isolation. Regeneration instructions in CMakeLists.txt (TPDE
section); CMake auto-enables AQP_HAVE_TPDE iff the .so exists.

New instrumentation: AQP_QJIT_TIME=1 (release-visible env, AQP_MP_TRACE
pattern) prints "[AQP-QJIT-TIME] codegen label=<L> us=<N>" around
CompileQuerySteps in TryCompileQueryJit — separates the
backend-compressible codegen part of the jit_compile CSV column from
Prepare/analysis. 6f none-split: codegen 26.0 ms (full O2+4passes) →
3.6 ms FastISel (7.1×) → 1.0 ms TPDE (26×), while the column floor
(~89 ms Prepare+analysis for that 5-join query) is backend-independent.
On node-based/rc sub-queries the floor is small ⇒ column ratios below.

Scripts: run_job.sh + measure_breakdown_time_job.sh arg 11 =
fast_compile (off|fastisel|tpde), filename suffix _fc<backend>.

