## Compensate-jit redesign IMPLEMENTED + MEASURED (2026-06-12, post-FASTALL)

User overrode the "shelved" verdict and ratified the §6.6 compensate-jit
redesign for implementation with keep-criterion "ANY query reduces
end-to-end time vs its best existing config". Plan file:
~/.claude/plans/hazy-munching-planet.md. VERDICT: KEPT — 25/113 queries
beat their best existing config (all via comptrue+fctpde).

### Semantics (as shipped)

`--compensate-jit[=true|false]` (param_config compensate_jit: -1 off /
0 false / 1 true; run_job.sh + measure_breakdown_time_job.sh arg 12,
filename suffix `_comp<v>`). Node-based only, all four jit levels
(expr/operator/pipeline/query). Flag absent = legacy spec behavior.
- EARLY LAUNCH: Phase-A bg compile launches at the POST-PREPARE hook
  (assumed_card = Prepare estimate) instead of post-execute, so the
  ALWAYS-full-quality bg compile overlaps Execute(i).
- USABILITY CHECK without completion (CheckSpeculativeResult): SQL
  match -> card guard (ratio<=2, hoisted BEFORE the wait; needs only
  temp_tables_, available at check time) -> only-if-usable wait
  (charged to next jit_compile column) -> bg_ok. Unusable specs are
  RetirePendingSpec()'d (zombie-park — bg may still be running),
  never waited on.
- MISS POLICY at the ExecuteSubIR check site (also fires for
  learned-miss skipped iterations via spec_learned_miss_iter_):
  true => inline recompile with the fast tier (adapter one-shot
  SetCompensateMissAction(FAST_ONCE): RegisterJITImpl/TryCompileQueryJit
  through EnsureFastJitCompiler = --fast-compile backend if set, else
  implied TPDE; separate compiler instance, F0/F1/F2 cache-key safe,
  own qjit-symbol registration guard); false => interpret immediately
  (duckdb levels: SetJITFlags only, no SetJITPendingIR; query-jit:
  SKIP_QJIT_ONCE skips TryCompileQueryJit, Prepare still runs, jit CSV
  column stays rectangular).
- NO Phase-B relaunch: on peek mismatch learn miss + retire, return
  before the relaunch block. Strictly one bg compile in flight
  (1-worker pool unchanged). Keep-on-match + is_final retirement stay.
- EnsureSpecCompiler forces the FULL backend for spec compilers in
  compensate mode (legacy keeps GetFastCompile()).
- Iteration 0 + final sub-query untouched (never spec-covered; they
  compile per the global --fast-compile as before).
- none-jit strict no-op (user invariant): enforced by the existing
  level gate in LaunchSpeculativeCompile — verified by stderr+result
  diff of 13a node-based --jit-level=none with/without the flag
  (identical modulo ASLR pointers).

### Early-launch race fix (deviation from the planned snapshot approach)

SpeculativeQueryJitCompile reads live temp maps under a "launches are
post-execute" assumption that early launch breaks (Execute(i) stores
temp(i) concurrently). Instead of copying map state into
SpeculativeCompilation, a single `std::shared_timed_mutex
temp_state_mutex_` (C++14 — project default; std::shared_mutex is
C++17) now guards the temp maps: shared locks at the bg read sites
(TempCollectionReplacementScan/TempCollectionBind/QjitTempBind/
RebuildTempTableIndices-in-spec) and exclusive locks at the 4
main-thread map-shape writer sites (qjit run-result insert, store
block qjit+CDC branches, RegisterPlaceholderTemp, GetOrLoadQjitTemp).
Simpler and covers interpreter-consumer binds too.

### BUG found by goldens: empty-placeholder bloom filters (FIXED)

First golden run: node-based pipeline x compensate {true,false} FAILED
on 9 queries (16a 17e 24b 26a 29c 30c 31c 9b 9c) with NULL results
(query-jit passed everywhere). Root cause: LaunchSpeculativeCompile
appends a temporary temp_tables_ entry for temp(i) so
ApplyCrossSubPlanOptimizations can derive range preds + bloom filters
from it — valid at post-execute launch (data complete), but at the
compensate-mode post-Prepare launch temp(i) is still the EMPTY
placeholder: BuildBloomFilter built a zero-row BF that rejected every
probe row on spec HIT (BFs are not in the SQL text, so the SQL-match
guard cannot catch them; query-jit was immune because its spec builds
no BFs and its range-pred divergence IS caught by the SQL match). Also
poisoned temp_min_max_cache_ ("temps are immutable" assumption). Fix:
in compensate mode don't append temp(i) — spec SQL then diverges from
inline SQL whenever the inline path injects temp(i)-derived range
preds, which routes to the miss policy by design (range constants are
baked into compiled code, never patchable). After fix: all 6 golden
configs pass (node-based query/pipeline x true/false + rc/none query
comptrue inertness runs).

### Timer re-audit (one-off WALL patch, reverted)

RunBenchmark per-iter wall vs CSV row sums under comptrue (10a/13a/
26b/6f, repeat=5): coverage 97.6-99.6%, gap 0.5-3.3 ms/iter = fixed
untimed overhead (result handling, log writes), NOT the hidden-wait
class. CSV accounting is sound under compensate mode.

