## Archived from CLAUDE.md §6.6 (2026-06-13 cleanup)

The following text was moved here from CLAUDE.md to keep that file
focused on current state and open work. This is the original §6.6
historical/design context that informed the fast-compile + compensate-jit
decisions.

### Original problem + model

Node-based + query-jit was COMPILE-dominated — suite compile 7.04 s vs
exec 4.56 s (nospec, warm). Two complementary attacks: HIDE compile
behind execution (speculation) or SHRINK it (fast backend). The model
said shrink first; measurement confirmed it.

### Terminology

- *Fast tier* = a fast-but-lower-quality JIT BACKEND for query-jit
  compiles. Two candidates: (1) LLVM `CodeGenOptLevel::None` + FastISel
  — same module, skip mid-end opt, quick instruction selector; no new
  dependency; expected ~2-5× faster compile, ~1.2-2× slower code.
  (2) TPDE — a separate single-pass compiler framework that consumes
  LLVM IR but bypasses LLVM codegen entirely (vendored in lingo-db at
  `/home/pei/Project/lingo-db/vendored/tpde/`, needs LLVM 19/20; we
  link 20.1 — compatible); ~10-20× faster than even LLVM -O0 at
  ~O0-quality code, but a new vendored dependency. Lingo-db ships both
  (cheap-LLVM backend: LLVMBackends.cpp:248). Incremental compilation
  is NOT feasible in LLVM (only constant patching — unusable here).
- *FASTALL* = POLICY: fast-compile EVERY sub-query inline on the
  critical path (incl. the final sub-query), no speculation, no
  background thread, no prediction. Flag: `--fast-compile`.
- *Compensate-jit* = POLICY: a bg thread always compiles sub-query i+1
  (full quality) DURING Execute(i), launched at post-Prepare(i+1) from
  estimated cardinality; at execute(i)-done check usability. On
  usability miss: `compensate=false` => drop, interpret immediately, no
  wait; `compensate=true` => fast-tier recompile. Usable-but-NOT-READY
  => wait. NEW_TRUE/NEW_FALSE in the table = these two settings.
- *REFINED* = compensate=true PLUS cost-based wait-vs-interpret on
  usable-but-NOT-READY events. Modeled as an ORACLE — not implementable.

### Instrumentation fix (LANDED 2026-06-12)

Mechanics in lingo-db-reuse.log "Spec-jit redesign decision". Spec-jit's
blocking waits fell in an untimed CSV gap => every node-based spec-ON CSV
measured before 2026-06-12 underreports wall time by the full wait (10a
37.8->75.3 ms, 26b 73.1->125.6 ms). Now waits are charged into the next
jit_compile column, and --debug runs emit per-iter [AQP-SPECJIT] traces.
rc/none splits were never affected (no spec there).

### Full-JOB model (113 queries)

Node-based + query-jit, warm medians; inputs: nospec/spec/interp sweeps
repeat=15 + --debug trace repeat=3; fast tier ASSUMED 10x compile / 1.5x
exec unless noted. Everything archived SELF-CONTAINED in
`measure/notes/specjit_model_2026-06-12/`.

| design | compile | exec | overhead | TOTAL | beats interp |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| INTERP (measured) | 0.00 | 10.23 | 0.72 | 10.95 s | - |
| NOSPEC (measured) | 7.04 | 4.56 | 0.83 | 12.43 s | - |
| CURRENT spec-jit (measured, fixed CSV) | 6.95 | 4.54 | 0.91 | 12.40 s | 39/113 |
| OLD (early launch + full relaunch) | 7.72 | 4.54 | 0.91 | 13.18 s | 35/113 |
| NEW_FALSE (compensate=false) | 2.72 | 7.69 | 0.91 | 11.32 s | 33/113 |
| NEW_TRUE (compensate=true) | 2.83 | 5.84 | 0.91 | 9.58 s | 69/113 |
| REFINED (oracle) | 2.47 | 5.42 | 0.91 | 8.80 s | 83/113 |
| FASTALL (no spec) | 0.70 | 6.84 | 0.83 | 8.38 s | - |
| FASTALL + oracle jit-vs-interp | - | - | - | 7.38 s | - |
| TRIPLE ORACLE | 0.61 | 4.86 | 0.83 | ~6.3 s | - |

### Verdict (key facts)

- CURRENT spec-jit ~ NOSPEC (12.40 vs 12.43 s): saves ~nothing.
- OLD (early launch, full-cost relaunch on miss): 13.18 s — worse.
- Usability under EARLY launch: 57/565 usable; 395 miss.
- FASTALL (8.38 s) beats even oracle-REFINED (8.80 s) at 10x/1.5x.
- Hybrid headroom: TRIPLE ORACLE 0.61 s compile + 4.86 s exec.

### Dependencies (why this order)

NEW_TRUE's miss path REQUIRES a fast backend. Both NEW designs need the
early-launch redesign + a 2nd compile worker. The model ignores core
oversubscription during overlap (~1/n exec slowdown). All fast-tier rows
rest on ASSUMED ratio/penalty => measure first.

### Sensitivity analysis

Ratio 5-20x x penalty 1.2-2.0: REFINED 8.24-9.73 s (always best spec
design, always beats interp); NEW_TRUE 8.80-11.21 s; FASTALL degrades to
~11.4 s at 5x/2.0. Cost model WITHOUT any fast tier: 11.04 s — loses to
interp => the fast tier is the decisive lever.

