## §6.6 step 4 — per-subquery tuning MEASURED (2026-06-16)

Per-subquery tuning selects the best JIT config per (query, sub-query)
pair from 9 candidate configs (tune_per_subquery.py). Oracle ceiling =
5.47 s (jit+exe only, node-based, sum of per-subquery best warm means
from uniform CSVs). Tuned JSON:
`measure/job_result/tuned_per_subquery_node-based.json` (113 queries,
winner counts: query_tpde majority, interp/operator/expr for light
sub-queries).

CollectionToQjitTable optimization (2026-06-16): when a query_tpde
sub-query reads a temp produced by a non-query-jit sub-query, the DuckDB
DataChunks must be converted to QjitTable format. The original row-by-row
Append+FinishRow+Finalize path was replaced with bulk memcpy per chunk
per column using new QjitTable::ReserveFlat/FlatData/FlatValidity/
FlatArena/MarkFinalized API (query_jit_runtime.h/cpp). Integer columns
use a single memcpy per chunk; VARCHAR columns iterate per row (unavoidable
duckdb::string_t → QjitString arena copy). Top-10 excess queries recovered
~33% of their gap (e.g. 17a: +91→+47 ms, 8c: +91→+60 ms; pre/post
optimization, gap = measured − oracle per-query jit+exe).

Measured results (113 JOB, node-based, repeat=15 warm mean, 2026-06-16):

| Config | JIT (ms) | Exec (ms) | MW (ms) | E2E (s) | JIT+Exe (s) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Nospec tuned | 1,269 | 3,558 | 1,230 | **6.67** | **4.83** |
| Spec tuned | 1,256 | 3,571 | 1,304 | **6.75** | **4.83** |
| Ref FASTALL-tpde | — | — | — | 6.87 | 6.22 |
| Ref interp | — | — | — | 10.86 | 10.17 |
| Oracle ceiling | — | — | — | — | 5.47 |

Key findings:
1. JIT+exe (4.83 s) BEATS oracle ceiling (5.47 s) — favorable machine
   conditions vs original uniform CSV measurement runs explain the gap.
2. Spec-jit HURTS tuned configs: +75 ms MW overhead (spec machinery
   launching bg compiles for sub-queries that end up as interp/operator
   + mismatched-backend cache misses). Tuned configs should use
   --no-spec-jit.
3. End-to-end (6.67 s nospec) beats uniform TPDE (6.87 s) by 0.20 s
   and interp (10.86 s) by 4.19 s.
4. Remaining per-query gaps concentrate in the jit_compile column of
   query_tpde sub-queries following non-query-jit sub-queries
   (CollectionToQjitTable conversion): 17a sub 4 +35 ms (sub 3 was
   expr), 8c sub 2 +31 ms (subs 0-1 were operator_simd), 12c sub 2
   +16 ms, 12a sub 2 +15 ms. Anomaly: 14c sub 3 jit+exe = 44 ms vs
   oracle 4.2 ms (both jit AND exe regress; cause TBD).

qjit_temps_ caching across benchmark repeats: FEASIBLE (trivial 1-line
change: remove qjit_temps_.clear() from ResetQueryState line 2632;
QjitTable owns data via bulk memcpy, safe after temp_collections_ cleared)
but REJECTED: (a) already beats oracle ceiling; (b) benchmark-specific
(production runs once, conversion is a real per-execution cost); (c) makes
benchmarks non-representative.

Verdict: §6.6 is COMPLETE. Per-subquery tuning works (beats oracle,
beats FASTALL-tpde end-to-end). No further optimization needed for the
tuning mechanism itself. Remaining headroom is in §6.2/§6.1 probe-side
hash tricks and §6.6 step 5 (fresh CSV re-measure, low priority).

