### §6.11 Query-jit ROF two-phase split — COMPLETE (2026-06-18)

Implemented two-phase ROF (Run-Once-Filter) in CompileQuerySteps
(ir_to_llvm.cpp). Architecture: Phase 1 evaluates guard range checks +
pre-probe filters + murmur hash for first probe keys in a scalar loop,
writing (row_idx, hash) to stack selection buffer. Phase 2 iterates
the selection buffer, skips already-evaluated guards/filters, and uses
precomputed hash for the first HT probe.

Key decisions:
- Reuses --jit-simd=auto flag (no new flag). Activated when use_simd_ &&
  vec_width > 1 && compile_mode != TPDE && first probe keys all from
  source columns.
- Handles both block-skip and non-block-skip loop variants (block-skip
  emits the outer block loop + inner ROF loop; non-block-skip emits
  ROF directly over [begin, end)).
- TPDE falls back to original scalar loop (ROF uses LLVM IR constructs
  that TPDE's register allocator can't optimize).
- Cache key already includes simd_isa_, so ROF decision is captured.
- Stack buffers: rof_sel_buf (i32 array, morsel-sized), rof_hash_buf
  (i64 array, morsel-sized). Allocated on function entry.
- Guards and pre-first-probe filters skipped in Phase 2 via
  `if (use_rof && oi < rof_first_probe) continue` pattern.
- Precomputed hash loaded from rof_hash_buf in Phase 2, replacing
  the inline murmur computation for the first probe.

Correctness: 113 JOB queries × 3 splits (none, node-based,
relationship-center) PASS against golden files (2026-06-18).
TPDE fallback verified correct (scalar path, same results).
