### §7.4 Performance analysis: single-run improvement ceiling (2026-06-24)

Not an implementation task. Quantitative analysis behind §7.2 and §7.3.

Baseline (TPDE, node-based, 10 warm runs, 113 JOB queries):
- Overhead: 0.66 s, JIT compile: 0.57 s, Execute: 5.04 s, Total: 6.26 s
- Single-thread phases: 1.23 s (19.6% of total)
- Multi-thread phases (12 workers): 5.04 s (80.4%)

Key findings:
1. Query-jit already uses 12 threads (hardware_concurrency()).
   QjitWorkerPool spawns 12 persistent workers with morsel-parallel
   scheduling (query_jit_executor.cpp:15-18). No additional thread
   parallelism available for execution.
2. Query-jit-only per-sub-query tuning (picking from TPDE/fastisel/
   LLVM O2/SIMD per sub-query) saves only 0.11 s over pure TPDE.
   TPDE wins 641/678 sub-queries; compile cost of LLVM O2 (~12 ms)
   outweighs its exe savings for most sub-queries.
3. Mixed-config tuning (interp+query-jit) produces CDC conversion
   cost (540 ms) that exceeds execution savings (429 ms), making the
   tuned config 220 ms SLOWER than pure TPDE.
4. The 20% improvement target (5.01 s) is NOT achievable within a
   single first run. Maximum single-run savings: pipeline (392 ms) +
   cache (142 ms) + qjit-only tuning (110 ms) = 644 ms → 5.62 s
   (10.3% improvement).
5. The 20% target IS achievable with persistent cache: 2nd run with
   LLVM O2 cached objects → 4.70 s (24.9% improvement).

Config performance table (node-based, 113 JOB queries):

| Config | Overhead | JIT | Exe | Total |
|--------|----------|-----|-----|-------|
| interp | 0.69 s | 0.00 s | 10.32 s | 11.01 s |
| query_tpde | 0.66 s | 0.57 s | 5.04 s | 6.26 s |
| query_fastisel | 0.66 s | 1.23 s | 5.37 s | 7.26 s |
| query_full (LLVM O2) | 0.67 s | 7.55 s | 4.03 s | 12.25 s |
| tuned (mixed) | 0.73 s | 1.15 s | 4.61 s | 6.48 s |

